<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details.</span>
We train on 8×A800 (80GB) GPUs and perform real-world inference on a single RTX 5090 (32GB) GPU. For simulation, we build on OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite> as the backbone. For the LIBERO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, the network is fine-tuned using Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) with rank 32 and scaling factor <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=64</annotation></semantics></math> for 60K optimization steps, with a batch size of 64 and an initial learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We set the action chunk length to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math>. We evaluate checkpoints every 10K steps on the validation benchmarks and report results using the checkpoint that achieves the best performance. For the RoboTwin 2.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>, we set a batch size of 32, an initial learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an action chunk size of 25. As for the Region and World tokens, we set their numbers to 64 and 9, respectively.
For real-world experiments, we adopt an action chunk length of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=25</annotation></semantics></math> and fine-tune OpenVLA using LoRA with rank 32 and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=64</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is trained for 80K steps with a batch size of 32, starting from a learning rate of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is reduced to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\times 10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\times 10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> after 50K steps. Beginning at step 60K, we evaluate checkpoints every 10K steps and use the best-performing checkpoint.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For selecting manipulable regions, we apply a speed threshold <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to generate a binary motion mask, and set <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For depth supervision, we use monocular depth estimators, specifically Depth-Anything v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite>, to generate pseudo-ground-truth depth labels. For semantic information, we adopt SAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> and use the output of its image encoder as dense, segmentation-aware features.
For the LWVQ, we utilize the C-ViViT model architecture from Villegas <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> to replicate the model from GENIE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>.
We use a quantization approach for the world knowledge variation codebook with a codebook size of 8 and a quantization dimension of 32.
During training, we use a batch size of 64 and set the learning rate to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> for a total of 30K steps.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Benchmark.</span>
We conduct extensive evaluations on two simulation benchmarks:
(1) Four task suites of <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LIBERO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>—Spatial, Object, Goal, and Long</span>, each with 10 tasks and 50 demonstrations; and
(2) Eight task configurations from the <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RoboTwin 2.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite></span>. LIBERO features substantially richer natural-language instructions than RLBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite>, making it a challenging testbed for language grounding and multimodal reasoning. For LIBERO, we evaluate each task over 50 trials.
RoboTwin 2.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> provides a large-scale and highly diverse benchmark for evaluating bimanual robotic manipulation under realistic and domain-randomized settings. We select eight tasks for evaluation: 1) Click Alarmclock, 2) Open Laptop, 3) Place Container Plate, 4) Dump Bin Bigbin, 5) Press Stapler, 6) Click Bell, 7) Stack Bowls Two, and 8) Place Bread Skillet. Each task is evaluated over 100 trials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-World Experiments.</span> We deploy <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>VLA on both the AgileX Cobot Magic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite> and Galaxea R1 Lite platforms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> for real-world evaluations. We conduct four long-horizon tasks: 1) Drawer Manipulation, 2) Align Shoes, 3) T-shirt Folding, and 4) Plate Lemon Apple. Each task contains 50 human-teleoperated demonstrations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baselines.</span> We compare <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>VLA with a range of state-of-the-art methods, including general VLA models such as OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, OpenVLA-OFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, STAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and PD-VLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite>.
In addition, we evaluate against predictive-paradigm approaches such as CoT-VLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, Seer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, MDT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>, UniVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite>, <math id="S5.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, and DreamVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>,
which explicitly incorporate future subgoal imagery or world-knowledge foresight into action reasoning. For real-world experiments, we reproduce representative models including OpenVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite>, OpenVLA-OFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, Seer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, and DreamVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>,
using the same deployment settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall Performance</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulation Experimental Results.</span>
We evaluate <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>VLA on two challenging simulation benchmarks, LIBERO and RoboTwin 2.0.
As reported in Tables <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Experimental Setup ‣ V Experiments ‣ ΔVLA: Prior-Guided Vision-Language-Action Models via World Knowledge Variation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-A Experimental Setup ‣ V Experiments ‣ ΔVLA: Prior-Guided Vision-Language-Action Models via World Knowledge Variation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>,
<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>VLA achieves a success rate of 97.8% on LIBERO and 80.4% on RoboTwin 2.0, establishing new state-of-the-art performance across diverse task suites.
Compared to predictive paradigms such as CoT-VLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> and DreamVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, which learn to forecast future states and then derive actions from the predicted outcomes, <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>VLA yields consistently higher success rates across both benchmarks.
This suggests that simply generating plausible future observations is not sufficient for robust control, especially when the predicted futures are weakly grounded in the current interaction context.
In contrast, our method explicitly models world-knowledge variations that capture action-induced, decision-relevant changes, which provides a more direct learning signal for manipulation policies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>VLA also outperforms non-predictive VLA baselines such as OFT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite>, indicating that the gains are not merely attributed to introducing an auxiliary prediction objective.
Instead, the improvements stem from reformulating prediction as learning task-relevant variations and aligning them with action generation.
Overall, <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math>VLA bridges the gap between perception and control more effectively than prior approaches, leading to stronger robustness under environment variations and better generalization.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE III</span>: </span><span id="S5.T3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Real-World Results.<span id="S5.T3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> “†” indicates results reproduced using the same settings as <math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝚫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>VLA. The table shows success rates for the progressive stage across four long-horizon tasks, with the average success rate representing only the completion of the entire task.</span></span></figcaption>
